1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,037
Lesson 7.4.

2
00:00:02,417 --> 00:00:04,973
The diffusion order-finding theorem.

3
00:00:06,083 --> 00:00:08,605
We already saw that
heat on the Cayley graph

4
00:00:08,605 --> 00:00:11,412
spreads until it equals
one over r at every node.

5
00:00:11,792 --> 00:00:15,107
The question is how many
steps are needed to read r

6
00:00:15,107 --> 00:00:17,966
without error, without
knowing r in advance.

7
00:00:18,346 --> 00:00:22,284
The theorem of Cadavid and
colleagues answers: a number of steps

8
00:00:22,284 --> 00:00:25,790
that grows only as the square
of the number of bits of N.

9
00:00:26,917 --> 00:00:31,155
Let M = ⌊log₂ N⌋ + 1.

10
00:00:31,535 --> 00:00:36,850
For every ℓ ≥ 4(M + 1)(log₂ N + 2),

11
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:43,715
the value of the heat kernel at the
identity is within 1/(4N²) of one over r.

12
00:00:44,095 --> 00:00:49,511
And since two distinct reciprocals
of integers smaller than N differ by

13
00:00:49,511 --> 00:00:54,088
at least 1/N², rounding one
over that value gives exactly r.

14
00:00:54,468 --> 00:01:00,666
For N = 299, M = 9 and the bound
is four hundred nine steps.

15
00:01:01,783 --> 00:01:04,411
The key piece is the doubling lemma.

16
00:01:04,791 --> 00:01:11,988
If 2^M > r, for every nonzero
frequency k there exists t ≤ M

17
00:01:12,368 --> 00:01:18,844
such that k 2^t mod r falls in the
central interval, between r/4 and 3r/4.

18
00:01:19,224 --> 00:01:22,951
The reason: as long as it does
not fall there, the distance to

19
00:01:22,951 --> 00:01:26,316
zero doubles at each step,
and it cannot double forever.

20
00:01:26,696 --> 00:01:30,809
With r = 33 and k = 1:
one, two, four, eight,

21
00:01:30,809 --> 00:01:34,648
sixteen; sixteen is already in the center.

22
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:41,953
In the central interval, the cosine
of 2πk2^t/r is negative or zero.

23
00:01:42,333 --> 00:01:46,143
So in the average of M
+ 1 cosines, at least

24
00:01:46,143 --> 00:01:49,346
one does not add up: μ_k ≤ M/(M + 1),

25
00:01:49,726 --> 00:01:53,994
and λ_k ≤ 1 − 1/(2(M + 1)).

26
00:01:54,374 --> 00:01:58,080
For M = 9, the bound is 0.95.

27
00:01:58,460 --> 00:02:02,957
It is a single bound for all
frequencies: a lacunary exponential

28
00:02:02,957 --> 00:02:06,821
sum, with doubling jumps,
cannot stay near its maximum.

29
00:02:07,950 --> 00:02:09,086
Now everything fits.

30
00:02:09,466 --> 00:02:16,919
The error is at most the largest λ_k^ℓ,
which is smaller than e^(−ℓ/(2(M + 1))).

31
00:02:17,299 --> 00:02:25,078
With ℓ_0 steps, that is smaller
than e^(−4)/N², below 1/(4N²).

32
00:02:25,458 --> 00:02:28,645
The order is determined
by a single readout,

33
00:02:28,645 --> 00:02:31,760
after a number of steps
of order (log₂ N)².

34
00:02:32,883 --> 00:02:34,401
The bound is conservative.

35
00:02:34,781 --> 00:02:38,593
For N = 299, rounding
already gives thirty-three

36
00:02:38,593 --> 00:02:42,324
from step seventeen,
against four hundred nine.

37
00:02:42,704 --> 00:02:45,728
For N equal to one million
twenty-two thousand one hundred

38
00:02:45,728 --> 00:02:49,065
seventeen, with order five thousand
three hundred thirteen, step

39
00:02:49,065 --> 00:02:52,505
forty-six suffices, against one
thousand eight hundred forty-five.

40
00:02:52,885 --> 00:02:56,401
But beware: each step
updates r nodes at once.

41
00:02:56,781 --> 00:02:59,590
If simulated digitally,
it costs r operations

42
00:02:59,590 --> 00:03:01,962
per step and the advantage disappears.

43
00:03:02,342 --> 00:03:05,572
The theorem shifts the
cost from time to physical

44
00:03:05,572 --> 00:03:08,537
resources, which we will
study in lesson 7.6.

45
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:16,384
The full algorithm: pick a, move to the
odd base, diffuse, read and round, recover

46
00:03:16,384 --> 00:03:22,626
the order of a with a few doublings and
extract the factor as in lesson 7.1.

47
00:03:23,006 --> 00:03:26,199
In lab ten you will run it end to end.

48
00:03:27,317 --> 00:03:30,289
Common mistakes: claiming
there is a polynomial

49
00:03:30,289 --> 00:03:33,388
digital algorithm, when
the vector has r entries.

50
00:03:33,768 --> 00:03:36,375
Forgetting the condition
of the doubling lemma.

51
00:03:36,755 --> 00:03:41,338
Ignoring that the readout needs
about two log₂ N bits of precision.

52
00:03:41,718 --> 00:03:44,550
And reading the bound as
the typical number of steps.

53
00:03:45,650 --> 00:03:50,420
In summary: the doubling lemma
guarantees that every nonzero frequency

54
00:03:50,420 --> 00:03:55,530
eventually falls in the negative-cosine
zone, which bounds all eigenvalues.

55
00:03:55,910 --> 00:04:03,720
After ℓ_0 = O((log N)²) steps, rounding
one over p_ℓ(e) gives the order.

56
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:07,382
In practice far fewer steps
suffice, but each step

57
00:04:07,382 --> 00:04:10,205
costs physical resources
proportional to r.

58
00:04:11,317 --> 00:04:15,240
To practice: apply the doubling
lemma to another frequency.

59
00:04:15,620 --> 00:04:18,087
Prove the separation between reciprocals.

60
00:04:18,467 --> 00:04:20,293
Compute the bound for another N.

61
00:04:20,673 --> 00:04:23,841
And explain what the theorem does
not say about energy and area.
